<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Todor Zhivkov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1911–1998</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Todor Zhivkov was the communist leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> for 35 years, first secretary of the Bulgarian Communist Party from 1954 to 1989 and head of state from 1971. He kept Bulgaria the most loyal Soviet ally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, oversaw rapid industrialisation and in the 1980s led a forced assimilation campaign against the country's Turkish minority. He was ousted by reformers in his own party on 10 November 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/todor-zhivkov.jpg" alt="Todor Zhivkov" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Extracted by Fredy.00 · CC BY-SA 3.0 cz · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Todor_Jivkov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
